\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{graphicx}

\title{Canopy Light Interception and Fruit Composition\\ in a Hillside Vineyard}
\author{E.~Branco and R.~Vasquez}
\date{June 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Canopy management in steep vineyards aims to balance light
interception against the risk of sunburn on exposed clusters.
We report a season of canopy imagery and fruit composition
measurements from a single hillside block.

\section{Site and Measurements}
The block is planted on a twelve percent slope with rows
oriented across the contour.
Canopy images were acquired at veraison and again three weeks
later, and fruit samples were collected from marked vines
at harvest.

\section{Canopy Structure}
The mid-season canopy map summarises the interception gradient
between the upper and the lower parts of the slope.
\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{figures/canopy_ndvi.png}
\caption{Mid-season canopy greenness map of the study block.}
\end{figure}
Interception at the top of the slope exceeded that at the
bottom by a wide margin throughout the measurement period.

\section{Fruit Composition}
Berries from the more exposed upper rows ripened earlier
and showed higher sugar accumulation at harvest,
while acidity remained similar across the slope positions.

\section{Conclusion}
A single vigour map explains most of the harvest gradient
and can guide selective picking in future seasons.
\end{document}

